neutron: winewayland: translate key events with the active layout (event thread follows the compositor's group)

Found while working Neutron#55 (@monkeysk4nk, Colemak-DH): his words "I noticed an issue with indesign it never seem to
have the correct layout, when typing or using ctrl + key." His single Colemak layout does not need this patch (the
letter-VK patch fixes his case); this one covers the same symptom family in the setups next to his.

Mechanism: winewayland sends keys as KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE and win32u's send_hardware_message() maps the scancode to a
VK with NtUserGetKeyboardLayout(0) of the CALLING thread (its own TODO: "Use the keyboard layout of the target hwnd").
That thread is winewayland's event thread; set_current_xkb_group() only posts WM_INPUTLANGCHANGEREQUEST to the focused
window, so the event thread keeps the locale's default HKL. When the active xkb group's HKL differs from it - a layout
whose language is not the user locale's (fr under en_US: 040C0409 vs 04090409) or the second of two layouts
(us + us(colemak_dh): F0010409) - KbdLayerDescriptor finds another layout's table or none (built-in US), while
TranslateMessage/ToUnicodeEx in the app use the active one. Result: AZERTY under en_US TYPED QWERTY letters (scan 0x1e
-> VK_A -> 'a', 0x2c -> 'z', 0x27 -> '$'), QWERTZ typed z for y and u-umlaut for o-umlaut, and in a US + Colemak
setup the Colemak group typed QWERTY text and kept QWERTY shortcuts. With LANG=fr_FR (HKL 040C040C) AZERTY was right.

Windows: "The system provides language-independent keyboard support by using the language-specific keyboard layout
currently selected by the user or the application. The keyboard device driver receives scan codes ... which are sent to
the keyboard layout where they are translated" (learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/inputdev/about-keyboard-input,
"Keyboard Input Model"); the VK comes from the active layout, not from a fixed one.

Change: when the active group's HKL changes, set_current_xkb_group() (event thread) also calls
NtUserActivateKeyboardLayout(hkl, 0) for its own thread, so scancodes are translated with the layout the compositor
has active. A no-op when the HKL equals the event thread's (one layout matching the locale: every QWERTY en_US user).

Not covered: an app that refuses WM_INPUTLANGCHANGEREQUEST keeps its old layout for WM_CHAR while events follow the
compositor (Windows would follow the app's thread); the win32u TODO itself (per-target-thread layout) is unchanged.
i386-unix winewayland.so not rebuilt in the lab.

Lab (agent kb55, probe kbt.exe, nested KWin 6.8.2, same build 11.10-204kc, kill = NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0, n=2 each):
- fr under en_US: on: scan 0x1e -> VK_Q 'q', 0x2c -> VK_W 'w', 0x27 -> VK_M 'm' (AZERTY right; Ctrl on the 0x2c key no
  longer fires Ctrl+Z); off: QWERTY letters (= plain 204, hash-identical).
- de under en_US: on: y/o-umlaut right; off: z/u-umlaut (= 204).
- us,us(colemak_dh) Alt+Shift toggle: on: US group QWERTY VKs, Colemak group VK_S/VK_Z + Ctrl+S/Ctrl+Z accelerators and
  's'/'z' typed; off: Colemak group QWERTY in both VKs and text.
- us, us(colemak_dh) single, ru,us toggle: hash-identical on/off.
Kill switch: NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0.
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
@@ -813,6 +813,25 @@
     if (hkl == keyboard_hkl) return;
     keyboard_hkl = hkl;
 
+    /* NEUTRON event-thread-hkl (Neutron#55): win32u turns our KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE input into a VK with the
+     * keyboard layout of the CALLING thread (send_hardware_message's TODO: "Use the keyboard layout of the
+     * target hwnd"), and this is the event thread, which kept the locale's default HKL. Whenever the active
+     * layout's HKL differed from it (a layout whose language is not the user locale's, e.g. AZERTY under
+     * en_US, or the second of two layouts, e.g. US + Colemak), keys were turned into VKs with another
+     * layout's table (or the built-in US one) while WM_CHAR used the active one: AZERTY typed QWERTY letters.
+     * On Windows the scancode is translated with the active layout of the foreground thread. Make the event
+     * thread's layout follow the compositor's active group. NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0 turns it off. */
+    {
+        static int enabled = -1;
+        if (enabled < 0)
+        {
+            const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL");
+            enabled = !(e && e[0] == '0' && !e[1]);
+        }
+        if (enabled && !NtUserActivateKeyboardLayout(hkl, 0))
+            WARN("Failed to activate %p on the event thread\n", hkl);
+    }
+
     pthread_mutex_lock(&text_input->mutex);
     ime = text_input->focused_hwnd == keyboard->focused_hwnd;
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&text_input->mutex);
